; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Inspirals
; CATEGORY: Spirals & Curves
; LEVEL:    Intermediate
; TAGS:     inspi, turtle geometry, angles, gcd, closed curves
; SUMMARY:  Keeps the step the same but turns a little MORE each time, which winds the turtle into knots that close up on themselves.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   A polyspiral grows its steps. An inspiral does the opposite trick:
;   the steps stay the same and the turning angle grows: 2 degrees,
;   then 9, then 16, and so on. The turtle curls tighter and tighter,
;   unwinds, and comes back to where it started, drawing a tangled
;   but symmetric knot. Four inspirals with different increases are
;   shown, each centered in its own quarter of the screen.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run. Then try  clearscreen inspi 6 0 11 100  and other
;   increases in the command center.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * the INSPI procedure from the classic book "Turtle Geometry"
;   * the angles repeat after 360 / gcd(increase, 360) steps, and
;     a little more arithmetic tells when the turtle gets home
;   * finding the middle of a drawing by recording every point
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Increases of 1, 2, 3, 4: which ones give the most arms?
;   * An increase of 90 or 120. Can you predict the picture?
;   * Start with a different first angle, like 40, and the same increase.
; ============================================================

to gcd :a :b
  if :b = 0 [output :a]
  output gcd :b remainder :a :b
end

; Walk the inspiral once with the pen up, remembering every point,
; so we can work out its middle. Then draw it for real, shifted so
; that its middle lands on (cx, cy).
to inspi :side :angle :inc :cx :cy
  ; The turns repeat after N = 360 / gcd(inc, 360) steps. During those
  ; N steps the turtle turns through some total angle; if that is not a
  ; whole number of circles, the next N steps are the same drawing
  ; turned round, so we keep going until the turning adds up to whole
  ; circles. Then the turtle is home, facing the way it started.
  localmake "n 360 / gcd :inc 360
  localmake "total remainder :n * :angle + :inc * :n * (:n - 1) / 2 360
  localmake "steps :n * ifelse :total = 0 [1] [360 / gcd :total 360]
  localmake "xs [] localmake "ys []
  penup home
  repeat :steps [
    forward :side right :angle + :inc * (repcount - 1)
    make "xs fput xcor :xs make "ys fput ycor :ys
  ]
  ; the middle of the box round all the points
  localmake "mx ((reduce "max2 :xs) + (reduce "min2 :xs)) / 2
  localmake "my ((reduce "max2 :ys) + (reduce "min2 :ys)) / 2
  penup setxy :cx - :mx :cy - :my setheading 0 pendown
  repeat :steps [
    setpencolor (list 128 + 120 * sin 3 * repcount 90 + 80 * cos 2 * repcount 200)
    forward :side
    right :angle + :inc * (repcount - 1)
  ]
end

to max2 :a :b
  output ifelse :a > :b [:a] [:b]
end
to min2 :a :b
  output ifelse :a < :b [:a] [:b]
end

to caption :x :y :text
  penup setxy :x :y setpencolor [200 200 210] label :text
end

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground [20 20 35]
setpensize 1.5
setlabelheight 14
inspi 9 0 7 -200 150
caption -260 15 [increase 7]
inspi 15 2 20 200 150
caption 140 15 [increase 20]
inspi 8 0 11 -200 -140
caption -260 -285 [increase 11]
inspi 16 40 30 200 -140
caption 140 -285 [increase 30]
